<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard III of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1452">1452</a>–<a href="/years/1485">1485</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Richard III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1483 to 1485, the last king of the House of York. As Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> he took the throne after his nephew Edward V was declared illegitimate, and Edward and his brother, the Princes in the Tower, disappeared. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth Field</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>'s army, and his remains were found under a car park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> in 2012.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england.jpg" alt="Richard III of England" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barthel ii · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_III_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard was born on 2 October 1452 at Fotheringhay Castle in Northamptonshire, the eleventh of the twelve children of Richard, 3rd Duke of York, and Cecily Neville. His childhood fell in the opening phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, in which the Yorkists, who supported his father, opposed the Lancastrians loyal to the crown. His brother Edward IV gained the throne in 1461 and made him Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> that year. Richard became the richest and most powerful noble in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and showed loyalty to Edward during Warwick's rebellion of 1470. He married Anne Neville on 12 July 1472 and governed the north for Edward as the dominant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Edward IV died on 9 April 1483 and Richard was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-protector">Lord Protector</a> for the 12-year-old Edward V. Robert Stillington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a>, is said to have told Richard that Edward IV's marriage to Elizabeth Woodville was invalid because of an earlier union with Eleanor Butler, which made Edward V and his siblings illegitimate. Richard accepted the throne on 26 June after a petition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s nobles and commons, and was crowned on 6 July 1483. Edward V and his brother Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, called the Princes in the Tower, disappeared from the Tower of London around August 1483.</p><p>In October 1483 a rebellion nominally led by his former ally Henry Stafford, 2nd Duke of Buckingham, failed. Richard set up the Council of the North, with his nephew John de la Pole as president, to keep the peace and settle land disputes; it lasted until 1641. His only son, Edward of Middleham, died on 9 April 1484.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 1485 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a> and his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasper-tudor">Jasper Tudor</a> landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> with French troops. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth Field</a> on 22 August Richard's army, believed to be substantially larger, met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s. Richard was killed, the last English king to die in battle, and Henry became Henry VII. His body was buried without ceremony in Greyfriars Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>.</p><p>Sources for his reign are largely partisan and none was written by someone who knew him closely, so the debate over his character continues. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a> made him the protagonist of Richard III. In 2012 a search for the lost Greyfriars site found a skeleton, which the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> identified through radiocarbon dating, trauma sustained at Bosworth and mitochondrial DNA. He was reburied in Leicester Cathedral on 26 March 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard III of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the English stage he appears in the prologue of Christopher Marlowe's The Jew of Malta, and Shakespeare's Othello, Macbeth and <b>Richard III</b> have characters of the type called the stage Machiavel.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-prince">The Prince · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard III</b> was killed fighting at Bosworth Field when Lord Stanley's forces turned against him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The exiles Alexander Stewart, Duke of Albany, and James Douglas, 9th Earl of Douglas, led cavalry from England against James III, with <b>Richard III</b>'s permission but not his support.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-22">22 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry Tudor was crowned at Westminster Abbey two months after defeating <b>Richard III</b> at Bosworth.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fotheringhay Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Bosworth Field site, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,-0.44&amp;year=1473" aria-label="Fotheringhay Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Richard III of England"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,-0.44&amp;year=1473">What was here in 1473? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
